Surfaced during `/plan-ceo-review` of the linked-challenge follow-ups plan (2026-07-09; `Docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-09-challenge-followups-plan.md`). The clock-rollback hardening landing in that plan flags a suspicious `activity_log` row (`is_clock_suspect`) after sync, but `logActiveChallengeDay` grants streak completion and calls `awardChallengeCompletion` (stars/rank) synchronously on-device at log-time — before the async sync round-trip can flag anything. A clock-rollback that fabricates one completed day succeeds and is rewarded immediately; the flag only prevents the exploit from being *repeated* (future `getDoneDates` reads exclude the flagged row), it doesn't claw back a reward already granted. Closing this fully means: when a flagged row is found to have triggered a completion, reverse `awardChallengeCompletion` via the existing delete-challenge reward-reversal mechanism, reverting challenge status back to active. Needs careful handling of "stars already spent/converted elsewhere." Effort: L (human) → M with CC+gstack. Priority: P3 — best-effort anti-cheat (detect-and-exclude, no clawback) is the accepted posture until this is picked up.

